WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.769 --> 00:00:05.705
Andreas Wickberg loves
taking the snowmobile

00:00:05.705 --> 00:00:08.441
to his house in the
icy reaches of Lapland.

00:00:08.441 --> 00:00:11.644
All four houses in his tiny
Arctic community

00:00:11.644 --> 00:00:13.480
rely on satellite internet

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:16.516
for 500 kroner a
month, about $50.

00:00:16.516 --> 00:00:19.019
Wickberg and his wife
can hole up at home

00:00:19.019 --> 00:00:21.688
with a satellite internet
connection fast enough to do

00:00:21.688 --> 00:00:24.691
work calls by day and
stream movies by night.

00:00:24.691 --> 00:00:28.795
On the morning of
February 24, 2022,

00:00:28.795 --> 00:00:31.698
he turned on his computer
and gulped down the news that

00:00:31.698 --> 00:00:35.435
Russian President Vladimir Putin
had begun an invasion of Ukraine.

00:00:35.435 --> 00:00:37.437
A little while later,
his neighbor came around

00:00:37.437 --> 00:00:39.305
asking to borrow his Wi-Fi code

00:00:39.305 --> 00:00:41.975
saying their internet
connection had dropped.

00:00:41.975 --> 00:00:44.878
Wickberg shared the code
and kept going at work.

00:00:44.878 --> 00:00:48.681
10 minutes later his
connection dropped, too.

00:00:48.681 --> 00:00:51.651
He checked. All four
modem lights were off.

00:00:51.651 --> 00:00:53.920
That meant it was no
longer communicating with

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:56.389
a hulking great
geostationary satellite

00:00:56.389 --> 00:01:00.693
that beams down 82 individual spots
across Europe and Northern Africa,

00:01:00.693 --> 00:01:03.696
more than 20,000 miles
above him in the sky.

00:01:03.696 --> 00:01:07.567
It turned out his was one tiny
part of the biggest public outage

00:01:07.567 --> 00:01:10.003
of satellite internet
ever recorded.

00:01:10.003 --> 00:01:13.540
More than 45,000
modems had to be replaced,

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:15.675
yet it would take 35 days for the

00:01:15.675 --> 00:01:17.577
global communications
company affected,

00:01:17.577 --> 00:01:21.114
Viasat, to even begin to say what
it thought had happened,

00:01:21.114 --> 00:01:24.884
and 75 days for any country
to point the finger officially.

00:01:24.884 --> 00:01:28.922 position:89%
Back on Earth, Wickberg knew instantly what had happened.

00:01:28.922 --> 00:01:30.156
It had to be Russia.

00:01:36.296 --> 00:01:38.898
A few hours earlier and
several time zones away

00:01:38.898 --> 00:01:41.301
on an overcast evening
in Austin, Texas,

00:01:41.301 --> 00:01:44.637
a senior Viasat official settled
in at home for the night.

00:01:44.637 --> 00:01:47.307
Automated alerts
suddenly started pinging

00:01:47.307 --> 00:01:50.343
indicating abnormal
activity in Ukraine.

00:01:50.343 --> 00:01:52.078
Modems across much of Europe

00:01:52.078 --> 00:01:54.881
were unable to make
an internet connection.

00:01:54.881 --> 00:01:58.218
Viasat workers tried to force
offline modems that seemed

00:01:58.218 --> 00:02:01.788
overwhelmed by malicious traffic,
but more kept coming.

00:02:01.788 --> 00:02:04.691
They were playing satellite
modem Whac-A-Mole.

00:02:04.691 --> 00:02:07.594
The real target was more
than a 1,000 miles

00:02:07.594 --> 00:02:11.598 position:56%
and half a continent away from
Andreas Wickberg’s Lapland hideaway.

00:02:11.598 --> 00:02:13.967
Satellite communications
that Ukraine's government,

00:02:13.967 --> 00:02:16.936
army and intelligence
rely on were down.

00:02:16.936 --> 00:02:20.240
This catastrophic communications
loss hampered their defenses.

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:24.544
Backup came later in the form of
Elon Musk's Starlink terminals.

00:02:24.544 --> 00:02:27.313
These were game changing,
but they left the country reliant

00:02:27.313 --> 00:02:29.782
on support from an
erratic billionaire.

00:02:29.782 --> 00:02:32.685
The debilitating Ukraine hack
was also a wake-up call

00:02:32.685 --> 00:02:34.554 position:58%
to the commercial satellite industry.

00:02:34.554 --> 00:02:38.324
Almost all of daily life
turns on satellites, even time.

00:02:38.324 --> 00:02:40.994
Commercial satellites
determine GPS coordinates

00:02:40.994 --> 00:02:44.097
that underpin digital clocks
and cash machines.

00:02:44.097 --> 00:02:45.865
Space is playing an
even bigger role in

00:02:45.865 --> 00:02:48.902
homes, transport, food supply,
military operations,

00:02:48.902 --> 00:02:50.703
banking and communications.

00:02:50.703 --> 00:02:53.039
The risks are only set to rise.

00:02:53.039 --> 00:02:56.809
Today, there are close to 5,000
active satellites on orbit.

00:02:56.809 --> 00:03:00.246
In the next ten years that
number could hit 100,000.

00:03:00.246 --> 00:03:04.751
While Andreas Wickberg
waited on one of the 45,000+

00:03:04.751 --> 00:03:07.820
replacement modems
Viasat was busy sending out,

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:10.924
he decided to mail a
faulty one to an expert.

00:03:10.924 --> 00:03:14.427
A week later, Ruben Santamarta
opened the package, thrilled.

00:03:14.427 --> 00:03:16.729
A leading light in a very
small global band

00:03:16.729 --> 00:03:20.366 position:56%
of cybersecurity researchers who
are obsessed with satellite systems.

00:03:20.366 --> 00:03:23.002
Santamarta immediately
pulled the modem apart

00:03:23.002 --> 00:03:24.904
and dumped what's called
the flash memory.

00:03:24.904 --> 00:03:28.174 position:97%
That's the part of the device that stores key rewritable data.

00:03:28.174 --> 00:03:30.376
In place of a series of words,
strings and numbers

00:03:30.376 --> 00:03:32.712
common to code and
working modems,

00:03:32.712 --> 00:03:34.447
he found orderly repetition.

00:03:34.447 --> 00:03:35.882
Far from a temporary glitch,

00:03:35.882 --> 00:03:39.219
the destructive pattern
indicated a malware attack.

00:03:39.219 --> 00:03:41.821
Viasat admitted the
attack was preventable,

00:03:41.821 --> 00:03:44.457
but said they hadn't had
the capability to stop it.

00:03:44.457 --> 00:03:45.959
An attacker, they said, had

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:57.270
In other words, they got
hacked pretty bad.

00:03:57.270 --> 00:03:59.973
It took four weeks for the
US intelligence community

00:03:59.973 --> 00:04:02.342
to conclude Russia's
military intelligence unit,

00:04:02.342 --> 00:04:04.777
the GRU, carried out the attack.

00:04:04.777 --> 00:04:08.314
The US then embarked on a six
week quiet diplomatic campaign

00:04:08.314 --> 00:04:10.216
to publicly blame it on Russia.

00:04:10.216 --> 00:04:12.952
An unusual step that made
some European countries,

00:04:12.952 --> 00:04:17.056 position:58%
including France and Germany,
nervous in case it made things worse.

00:04:17.056 --> 00:04:19.325
But the argument went that
this was the most important

00:04:19.325 --> 00:04:21.227
cyber attack of the
Russian invasion.

00:04:21.227 --> 00:04:23.029
One no one who
subscribed to the service

00:04:23.029 --> 00:04:24.897
could do anything to stop.

00:04:24.897 --> 00:04:26.933
And one that could happen again.

00:04:26.933 --> 00:04:29.202
Eventually, the European
Union went public

00:04:29.202 --> 00:04:31.704
with the US careful to go second,

00:04:31.704 --> 00:04:35.642
an effort to send a signal to
Russia that everyone was united.

00:04:35.642 --> 00:04:40.113
The final frontier is still vastly
underprepared for cyber attacks.

00:04:40.113 --> 00:04:42.782
Commercial satellite
companies are still too lax

00:04:42.782 --> 00:04:44.484
when it comes to security

00:04:44.484 --> 00:04:47.954 position:97%
and some government satellite systems were built 30, 40 years ago,

00:04:47.954 --> 00:04:50.356
making them
especially vulnerable.

00:04:50.356 --> 00:04:53.993 position:98%
Starlink's Ukraine service has already faced attacks of their own

00:04:53.993 --> 00:04:56.996
and Viasat says it faces
ongoing threats.

00:04:56.996 --> 00:04:59.265
Russia has publicly warned
since the invasion

00:04:59.265 --> 00:05:03.569 position:59%
it sees some commercial satellites
as legitimate targets for retaliation.

00:05:03.569 --> 00:05:05.371
The US intelligence
community says

00:05:05.371 --> 00:05:09.342
Moscow will seek weapons that
can identify, track and target

00:05:09.342 --> 00:05:12.045
US satellites at speed
during a conflict.

00:05:12.045 --> 00:05:14.147
And the attack, say experts,

00:05:14.147 --> 00:05:16.683
is super, super repeatable.

